WezTerm's shell integration: OSC 133 semantic zones, OSC 7 cwd reporting, and
OSC 1337 user vars. The script is terminal-agnostic and version-stable (upstream
states the sequences are "not wezterm specific"), self-guards (bash/zsh only,
interactive only, skips TERM=dumb/linux), and bundles bash-preexec verbatim, so
it has no external dependency and is safe to source on any terminal -- terminals
that don't grok the OSC sequences ignore them.

WHY VENDORED HERE (not shipped with the wezterm binary, not used from /etc):
  * The bundled wezterm runtime archive (pre_built/.../runtime/wezterm.tar.bz2)
    ships only the binaries + `wezterm shell-completion` (tab-completion). It
    does NOT ship wezterm.sh shell integration.
  * The only system copy is /etc/profile.d/wezterm.sh, which (a) requires a
    distro wezterm package we don't depend on, and (b) lives in non-user-writable
    space -- against this loadout's no-/etc, user-writable, quota-friendly design.
  * Owning the file makes integration deterministic across raw wezterm,
    tmux-in-wezterm, tmux-in-st, st/xterm, --dest-dir and shared-prefix installs.

HOW IT IS LOADED:
  bash/global/bashrc sources it (when LOADOUT_CFG_ENABLE_WEZTERM_SHELL_INTEGRATION
  is truthy) via loadout_find_wezterm_shell_integration in bash/functions.sh.
  Resolver order: explicit LOADOUT_CFG_WEZTERM_SHELL_INTEGRATION -> this vendored
  copy -> paths relative to the installed `wezterm` binary -> shared prefix.
  Never /etc. See docs/BASH.md and bash/global/README.md "Prompt & shell
  integration" for the full rationale.

